<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahib I Giray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1551">1551</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giray-dynasty">Giray dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sahib I Giray was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> from 1521 to 1524 and khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> from 1532 to 1551. Installed in Crimea with Ottoman support, he used Ottoman troops and firearms to strengthen the khan's power over the great clans and built up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a> as his capital, with its palace. He was deposed and killed in 1551 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> replaced him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devlet-i-giray">Devlet I Giray</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahib-i-giray.jpg" alt="Sahib I Giray" width="320" height="597" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.20,_p._077.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sahib I Giray was a son of the Crimean khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menli-i-giray">Meñli I Giray</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haci-i-giray">Hacı I Giray</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giray-dynasty">Giray dynasty</a>. His brothers included Mehmed I Giray, Saadet I Giray and Mubarak. In 1510–11 he accompanied his stepmother Nur Sultan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, and during Mehmed's reign he was imprisoned for a long time. Khans had by custom to be descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, as Sahib was.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> was unstable and alternated between pro- and anti-Russian khans. After the death of Mohammad Amin, the Russians imposed their vassal Shahgali, but in the spring of 1521 the anti-Russian faction invited Sahib and he entered Kazan. His brother Mehmed, then khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, had placed him there, and in the summer of 1521 the two raided Muscovy together, Sahib reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> and Vladimir and Mehmed the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. His request to Saadet for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> was refused. In spring 1524 he declared himself an Ottoman vassal, but a large Russian army under Ivan Belsky forced him to flee.</p><p>In Crimea Saadet imprisoned him, then freed him, and he served as Saadet's deputy, or kalga. In May 1532 Saadet gave up the throne and the Ottoman sultan appointed Sahib khan. Turkish troops came with him, and the Crimean nobles recognised him. His nephew İslâm I Giray rose against him in several revolts, and he drove him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perekop">Perekop</a> in 1537. He founded the new capital of Bakhchisarai, expanded the harbour of Gozlev, tried to weaken the great nobles by raising lesser ones and tried to settle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a>. He campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, joined Suleiman against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petru-rares">Petru Rareș</a> in 1538, and in 1546 his artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> fire defeated 10,000 Nogais near Perekop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1551 Suleiman ordered Sahib to march against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, much farther east than the Crimeans had ever gone. Sahib replied that his warriors were poorly equipped, and the sultan, doubting his loyalty, decided to replace him with his nephew Devlet. While Sahib was in the Taman peninsula, Devlet landed with 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and took Bakhchisarai. Sahib's army deserted and his son Emin was killed. Sahib was imprisoned at Taman and killed on Devlet's orders. All his children and grandchildren were put to death, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devlet-i-giray">Devlet I Giray</a> became khan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sahib I Giray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45,34&amp;year=1541" aria-label="Crimea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5359 574 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sahib I Giray"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5809 844h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5809 844h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45,34&amp;year=1541">What was here in 1541? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
